  "summary": "Cobweb-fine shawls knitted from goat down — warm as wool, light as breath, patterned like frost.",
  "description": "Orenburg knitters spin the down of local goats into yarn thinner than thread and knit it into pautinka — \"cobwebs\" — square shawls of snowflake medallions, fir-tree borders and festooned openwork corners that pull through a wedding ring yet warm a winter steppe. Documented from the late 1700s and exhibited from Paris to Chicago, the shawls are knit from the center outward on two needles by farm women who memorize the lattice of zigzags and teeth. Genuine down shawls remain heirlooms, tested by passing through a ring and refilling after washing.",
